    "slug": "why-use-keys-in-react",
    "title": "Why we use Keys in React?",
    "desc": "<p>In React, the <code>key</code> attribute is used to identify which items have changed, been added, or \nremoved from a list. Keys help React efficiently update and re-render components by maintaining \nthe identity of each list item across updates. Without keys, React would have to re-render every item \nin the list, even if only one item changes, which can lead to performance issues.</p>\n<h2>Efficient Updates and Rendering</h2>\n<p>React uses keys to identify which elements in a list have changed, been added, or removed. This enables React to optimize the rendering process by:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Avoiding re-rendering unchanged items.</strong><br/>\n<strong>2. Ensuring that updates are applied to the correct elements.</strong></p>\n<p><code>Avoiding Index as Key</code>\nUsing an index as a key can lead to issues when the list changes, such as reordering or adding/removing items, because it \nmay not uniquely identify the element&#39;s position.\n<code>Rather use IDs as key index</code></p>\n",
